      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the geometric properties of an ellipse and the concept of the foci of the ellipse. The foci of an ellipse are located at a distance of $c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}$ from the center, where $a$ is the semi-major axis and $b$ is the semi-minor axis. Here, $a = 5$ and $b = 4$, so $c = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = 3$.\n\nThe key insight is that the circle is tangent to the ellipse at exactly one point along the major axis. The closest point on the ellipse to a focus is the point where the line from the center of the ellipse to the focus is perpendicular to the major axis. This point is given by the coordinates $(c, \\pm b)$.\n\nIn this case, the point closest to the focus is $(3, 4)$ or $(3, -4)$. Since the circle is tangent to the ellipse, the radius of the circle is the distance from the focus to this closest point on the ellipse, which is 3 units.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the semi-major axis (a) and semi-minor axis (b)\na = 5\nb = 4\n\n# Calculate the distance from the center to the focus\nc = sp.sqrt(a**2 - b**2)\n\n# The radius of the circle is the distance from the focus to the closest point on the ellipse\nradius = c\nprint(radius)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe radius of the circle, which is tangent to the ellipse with the given major and minor axes, is $\\boxed{3}$.",
