      "text": "To find the radius of the circle that is tangent to the ellipse and centered at one of its foci, we start by determining the key parameters of the ellipse. The lengths of the major and minor axes are given as 10 and 8, respectively. Therefore, the semi-major axis \\(a\\) and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) are:\n\\[\na = \\frac{10}{2} = 5, \\quad b = \\frac{8}{2} = 4.\n\\]\nThe distance \\(c\\) from the center to each focus can be found using the relationship \\(c^2 = a^2 - b^2\\):\n\\[\nc^2 = 5^2 - 4^2 = 25 - 16 = 9 \\implies c = \\sqrt{9} = 3.\n\\]\nThis means the distance from the center of the ellipse to each focus is 3. The foci of the ellipse are located at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine the radius of the circle that is tangent to the ellipse and centered at one of the foci. A useful property of ellipses states that the sum of the distances from any point on the ellipse to the two foci is constant and equal to the length of the major axis, which is \\(2a = 10\\).\n\nConsider the point on the ellipse directly to the right of the focus at \\((3, 0)\\). The distance from this point to each focus is the same and can be calculated as follows. If the point on the ellipse is \\((3 + x, y)\\), then the distance from this point to the focus \\((3, 0)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(3 + x - 3)^2 + y^2} = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\nThe distance from this point to the other focus at \\((-3, 0)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-3 - x - 3)^2 + y^2} = \\sqrt{(-6 - x)^2 + y^2} = \\sqrt{(6 + x)^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\nThe sum of these distances is the length of the major axis:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} + \\sqrt{(6 + x)^2 + y^2} = 10.\n\\]\nSince the point \\((3 + x, y)\\) lies on the ellipse, it satisfies the ellipse equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{25} + \\frac{y^2}{16} = 1\\). However, for the purpose of finding the radius, we don't need the exact coordinates but the fact that the sum of the distances is 10. The radius of the circle is the distance from the focus to the point of tangency, which is simply the distance from the focus to the nearest point on the ellipse at the tangency point, which is just the distance between the focus and the point directly to the right of it along the line connecting the foci.\n\nThis distance is simply the radius of the circle, which is the distance from the focus to the semi-major axis, subtracting the distance from the point of tangency to the nearest point on the ellipse, which is 3 (the distance from the focus to the directrix at \\(x = 3\\)). Therefore, the radius of the circle is:\n\\[\n3 - 3 + 4 = 4.\n\\]\nThus, the radius of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]",
